ay to improve the  limit of detection for the gin assay and  detecting copy number alterations  so we utilized the cfdna fragment size  the median length of cfdna from healthy  tissue is typically around 167 base  pairs  the rosenfeld lab at the university of  cambridge published a study on utilizing  fragment size analysis to enhance the  detection of circulating tumor dna  they demonstrated that in cancer types  with typically higher fractions of  circulating tumor dna  such as breast or lung cancers  there were significantly higher  proportions of fragments shorter than  150 base pairs when compared to healthy  donors or cancer types with typically  low amounts of ct dna such as renal or  bladder cancers  this indicates that tumor-derived cfdna  fragments are on average shorter than  cfdna fragments derived from healthy  tissue  in current workflows samples are  typically not size selected prior to  sequencing  so there are large fractions of wild  type or normal fragments that are  processed alongside tumor fragments  there are multiple ways to perform size  selection either in silico using  paradigm sequencing or several methods  of physical separation  size selection increases the tumor  fraction of the sample and can enrich  signal for the detection of  tumor-specific variants  which can reduce sequencing depths and  costs and improve the limit of detection  in cancer patients enriching for  tumor-specific dna can be useful for  early disease detection  or for predicting disease progression in  patients with typically low amounts of  ct dna  this figure demonstrates the  representation of the cfdna fragments in  a cancer patient  the pool before size selection on the  left contains a large fraction of  wild-type cf dna fragments  which have a median length of around 167  base pairs  there are few tumor cf dna fragments  which have a median length less than 167  base pairs  as an example the tumor fraction of this  sample before size selection is 10  performin