uld  make the change to the cob size to meet  our target  i provided only healthy libraries that  were generated from cfdna extracted from  pooled plasma of many healthy  individuals  the libraries themselves were then  pooled and split into 12 size selection  reactions  the figure on the top right shows that  the libraries were extremely consistent  before size selection and had very low  variability after size selection  over 98 percent of reads were below 150  base pairs after size selection  and the cutoff size average was 137 base  pairs with a cv of 1.2 percent  since these samples were all pooled  plasma from healthy patients we wanted  to run a final experiment to introduce  real patient samples  and ensure we could keep the cutoff size  and cv low  as well as keep a consistent fragment  size profile with minimal carryover  so in this final experiment we provided  more challenging samples from cancer  patients to verify the size cost and  consistency in a more realistic set of  patient samples  once again we saw over 98 of reeds below  150 base pairs and had an average cutoff  size of 135 base pairs with a cv of 2.2  percent  the results from this experiment showed  that the urging health size selection  instruments can reliably and accurately  size select our libraries  to summarize all five experiments which  included 58 samples from healthy  patients and 40 samples from cancer  patients libraries prior to size  selection had an average median cfdna  fragment size of 169 base pairs in  healthy patients  and 165 base pairs in cancer patients  after size selection the average median  fee of dna fragment sizes were  significantly lower  at 127 base pairs for healthy patients  and 120 base pairs for cancer patients  we also wanted to quantify copy number  alterations before and after size  selection  to see if we were enriching the signal  in cancer patients while maintaining the  signal for healthy patients  this figure shows a genome profile of a  healthy pa