uld  make the change to the cob size to meet  our target  i provided only healthy libraries that  were generated from cfdna extracted from  pooled plasma of many healthy  individuals  the libraries themselves were then  pooled and split into 12 size selection  reactions  the figure on the top right shows that  the libraries were extremely consistent  before size selection and had very low  variability after size selection  over 98 percent of reads were below 150  base pairs after size selection  and the cutoff size average was 137 base  pairs with a cv of 1.2 percent  since these samples were all pooled  plasma from healthy patients we wanted  to run a final experiment to introduce  real patient samples  and ensure we could keep the cutoff size  and cv low  as well as keep a consistent fragment  size profile with minimal carryover  so in this final experiment we provided  more challenging samples from cancer  patients to verify the size cost and  consistency in a more realistic set of  patient samples  once again we saw over 98 of reeds below  150 base pairs and had an average cutoff  size of 135 base pairs with a cv of 2.2  percent  the results from this experiment showed  that the urging health size selection  instruments can reliably and accurately  size select our libraries  to summarize all five experiments which  included 58 samples from healthy  patients and 40 samples from cancer  patients libraries prior to size  selection had an average median cfdna  fragment size of 169 base pairs in  healthy patients  and 165 base pairs in cancer patients  after size selection the average median  fee of dna fragment sizes were  significantly lower  at 127 base pairs for healthy patients  and 120 base pairs for cancer patients  we also wanted to quantify copy number  alterations before and after size  selection  to see if we were enriching the signal  in cancer patients while maintaining the  signal for healthy patients  this figure shows a genome profile of a  healthy patient with a couple of  probable germline copy number  alterations  the top portion shows the profile before  size selection where gin equals 94.3  and the copy number alteration on  chromosome 3 has an auc of 3.75  the bottom portion shows the profile  after size selection where gin equals  189.8  and that same copy number alteration has  an auc of 3.6  it's likely that the increase in noise  indicated by the wider band in the size  selected sample is causing gin to shift  above our threshold but that can likely  be mitigated by retraining the analysis  pipeline  alternatively this figure shows the  genome profile of a cancer patient  the top portion shows this patient's  profile before size selection  gin is over 1300 and one of the copy  number alterations on chromosome  you see of 35.8  after size selection is shown on the  bottom  gin nearly doubled to over 2500  and the same cna has an auc of 70.1  which is 1.95 times greater than before  size selection  this figure shows the auc ratios from  all cnas detected both before and after  size selection in all patients  an auc ratio value equal to 1 indicates  no difference in cna detection after  size selection  an auc value greater than 1 indicates an  increase in signal for the cna after  size selection and a value less than 1  indicates a decrease in signal  when evaluating cfdna from healthy  patients  30 cnas were found both before and after  size selection  the average auc ratio was 1.04  which is consistent with a lack of  signal enrichment in the absence of  disease  in cancer patients  237 cnas were detected before and after  size selection  and the average auc ratio was 1.99  which is consistent with an enrichment  of  signal a healthy patient with probable  germline cnas is shown in this example  panel a on the top left shows the  genome-wide profile of the sample before  size selection  which in equals 115.2  panel b on the bottom left shows the  genome wide profile of the sample after  size selection which in equals 164.5  panel c shows the fragment size profile  of the sample before and after size  selection  the median fragment length after size  selection was 138 base pairs whereas the  median before size selection was 170  base pairs and panel d shows the paired  analysis of the absolute value of the  auc for a total of four cnas detected  before and after size selection  the auc does not change significantly  after size selection indicated by an  average auc ratio of 1.19  this indicates that enrichment of  shorter cfdna fragments does not affect  signal from probable germline cnas  there also seem to be some cancer  patients where size selection does not  enhance the cna signal  the genome profiles before and after  size selection shows some larger cnas in  chromosomes 1 8 and 12 being the most  obvious  channel c shows that size selection was  successful with a median fragment size  of 166 base pairs before size selection  and 116 base pairs after size selection  there were four cnas detected both  before and after size selection in this  patient with an average auc ratio of  1.02  and we would likely need some additional  clinical data from these patients with  little or no enrichment to understand  the underlying causes for these patterns  and as we saw from the fragment size  profiles especially from experiment 2  there was some variability in the actual  cutoff sizes so these next figures show  samples with varying cutoff sizes  in this cancer patient sample we  achieved a size selection cutoff of 152  base pairs  panel a on the top left shows the  genome-wide profile of the sample before  size selection  origin equals 716.6  panel b on the bottom left shows the  genome-wide profile of the sample after  size selection  where cnas increased a small amount in  magnitude and the gin increased to  1201.5  channel c on the top right shows the  cfdna fragment size profile for the  sample before and after size selection  the median fragment length after size  selection was 148 base pairs whereas the  median before size selection was 167  base pairs  since the magnitude of the difference in  fragment sizes is relatively small there  is still considerable overlap in the  fragment size distribution between  conditions for this sample  panel d on the bottom right shows paired  analysis of the absolute value of the  auc for a total of 30 cnas detected  before and after size selection  there was significant enrichment of  cna's after size selection  indicated by an average auc ratio of 1.7  a lower size selection cutoff of 116  base pairs is shown in this cancer  patient example  panel a and b again show the genome-wide  profiles of the samples before and after  size selection  and you can visually see that cnas  increased significantly in magnitude and  the gin also increased significantly  to over 2300 after size selection  some examples of cnas can be seen on  chromosomes 2 and 7 where the entire  chromosomes are amplified after size  selection  channel c shows the cfdna fragment size  profile of this sample before and after  size selection the median fragment  length after size selection was 9  whereas the median before size selection  was 116 166 base pairs  since the magnitude of the difference in  fragment sizes is large there is very  little overlap in the fragment size  distribution between conditions for this  sample  panel d shows paired analysis of the  absolute value of the auc for a total of  18 cnas detected before and after size  selection  there was significant enrichment of  cna's after size selection indicated by  an average auc ratio of 3.7  so finally i will go over some  limitations from this study and our  conclusions from this instrument  evaluation  so one of the limitations of this study  was the auc calculation  it was a manual process and to be able  to accurately compare a specific cna  before and after size selection i  sometimes had to merge multiple bin  segments together and calculate an  overall auc for the merge segments  further method development they may be  necessary to indicate the significance  of those cnas  the second limitation while we had a  decent sized sample set with 58 healthy  samples and 40 cancer samples  each experiment had its own unique set  of parameters that were changed so  collecting more data using a consistent  protocol would give us more confidence  in our results  and the third limitation was that these  patients had various types of cancers in  different stages and different  treatments so digging into the clinical  data might allow us to draw some  additional conclusions  some overall trends we observed in this  data set include the median see if dna  fragment size significantly decreased  after size selection  additionally cnas increase in magnitude  after size selection for cancer patients  and not for healthy patients  lower cutoff sizes increase cna signal  further than higher cutoff sizes in some  cancer patients but this is not true for  all patients  and we do want to keep in mind a balance  between going shorter and having enough  material to sequence  and finally after troubleshooting with  your gene health we determined that size  selection using the nimbus select or the  light bench can  enhance the signal for the detection of  tumor-specific variants in cancer  patients  i want to thank my supervisor taylor  jensen for support and guidance  throughout this project  carrie fitzgerald helped with some of  the lab work for these experiments  jojun processed all of the sequencing  data and the overall lab court  bioinformatics team helped develop the  pipeline  jared and matt on the origin health team  provided or performed the size selection  on their instruments and were incredibly  helpful as we were troubleshooting the  process  and finally nate and graham provided me  with all of the samples used in this  study  thank you very much for attending my  talk and i am happy to take any  questions you have for me via email  thank you
 

